"""Replace only slides 41–45 using the instructor-supplied recent papers."""
def replace_recent(S):
    cook='Cook PF, Rouse AA, Sawyer E, Miller K, Berns G (2026). Seal and sea lion brains have evolved to support volitional control of vocal behavior and learning. Science 391(6790):1146–1150. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adx9367'
    sills='Sills JM, Ruscher B, Packard N, Jones RA, Southall BL, Reichmuth C (2025). Low-frequency hearing and masking parameters in representative seals and sea lions. Journal of the Acoustical Society of America 158(2):1585–1599. https://doi.org/10.1121/10.0039049'
    def put(n,title,cite,ref,doi,image,panel,caption,body,notes=()):
        paragraphs=body.split('|')
        S[n-2]={'layout':'figure-right','title':title,'body':paragraphs,'figure':{'path':'figures/'+image+'.png','kind':'article','caption':cite+', Fig. '+panel+'. '+caption,'source_url':'https://doi.org/'+doi},'cite':cite,'refs':[ref],'transcript':paragraphs+list(notes)}
    put(41,'Histology anchors vocal pathways in the sea-lion brain','Cook et al. (2026)',cook,'10.1126/science.adx9367','Cook2026_f1','1A–C','Histological and MRI localization of brainstem and cortical regions.',
      'Cook and colleagues combined tissue staining with **diffusion magnetic resonance imaging**, which measures direction-dependent water movement in preserved brain tissue. **Ex vivo** means outside the living animal. Histology located the **nucleus ambiguus**, a brainstem nucleus associated with control of muscles used in breathing, swallowing, and vocalization.|The stained sections were aligned with structural MRI using anatomical landmarks. Researchers marked the corresponding **voxels**, the three-dimensional sampling units of the scan. Cortical regions included primary auditory cortex, vocal motor cortex, and the neighboring vocal premotor region, which is associated with preparation of vocal actions.|**Probabilistic tractography** estimates possible fiber trajectories from diffusion measurements. The researchers traced pathways in both hemispheres and compared California sea lions, elephant seals, harbor seals, and coyotes. Combining microscopic identification with MRI constrained where each trace began and which anatomical regions a proposed route had to traverse.')
    put(42,'Vocal motor pathways reach the phonatory brainstem','Cook et al. (2026)',cook,'10.1126/science.adx9367','Cook2026_f2a','2A','Reconstructed sea-lion cortical–brainstem pathway in two views.',
      'All examined pinniped species had robust bilateral tractography connections between putative **vocal motor cortex** and nucleus ambiguus. Corresponding pathways were absent in the examined coyotes. Whole-brainstem and corticospinal control traces were comparable across species, separating the vocal-pathway difference from a general failure to reconstruct coyote fibers.|The vocal pathway was reconstructed while excluding the **periaqueductal gray**, a midbrain region involved in vocal control. This identified a route that could bypass that intervening region. Tractography estimates macroscopic pathways; its voxels cannot resolve whether the route contains a single synaptic connection.|The authors hypothesize that enhanced cortical access to phonatory brainstem circuits supports voluntary breathing and flexible use of existing calls. Submersion requires precise control over respiratory actions. Sea lions can learn when to produce or inhibit a species-typical call, linking this anatomical organization with contextual control of vocal behavior.',
      ['The reconstructed routes concern vocal and respiratory control. The study did not record the neural circuitry generating Ronan’s head-bob synchronization.'])
    put(43,'Voluntary calls and vocal imitation differ in circuitry','Cook et al. (2026)',cook,'10.1126/science.adx9367','Cook2026_f3def','3D–F','Forebrain pathway strengths in coyotes, sea lions, and seals.',
      '**Vocal usage learning** changes when an existing call is deployed. **Vocal production learning** changes the acoustic form of a call through experience. Sea lions exhibit flexible usage learning, whereas some true seals also learn new call forms or mimic sounds outside their usual repertoire.|Harbor seals had enhanced connectivity between the **anterior ventrolateral thalamus** and vocal premotor cortex. The study also traced pathways involving the **striatum**, a forebrain structure involved in action selection and reinforcement learning. The clearest forebrain difference involved the thalamic–premotor pathway, rather than uniformly stronger connectivity throughout every tested route.|Elephant and harbor seals additionally had robust auditory–premotor pathways that were not reconstructed robustly in sea lions. The authors hypothesize that these connections support developmental modification of calls using auditory input. Different combinations of pathways accompany different forms of vocal flexibility across closely related pinniped lineages.',
      ['The study compares anatomical connectivity with the behavioral literature. It did not train the scanned animals to acquire new vocalizations.'])
    put(44,'Underwater hearing extends to sounds near 40 Hz','Sills et al. (2025)',sills,'10.1121/10.0039049','Sills2025_f1','1a–b','Underwater hearing thresholds and background noise across frequencies.',
      'Sills and colleagues measured low-frequency underwater hearing in the California sea lion Ronan and bearded seals. The trained animals remained at a listening station and moved to a response target after detecting a sound. Signal-absent trials measured responses without a sound, while calibrated hydrophones measured the acoustic conditions.|An **audiogram** plots the sound level required for detection against frequency. Ronan detected a 40 Hz signal at a threshold of 121 dB re 1 µPa. This underwater pressure reference differs from the reference used for airborne hearing. Below 80 Hz, her threshold rose by only about 4 dB over the next octave.|At 40 Hz, the seals’ thresholds were 105 dB re 1 µPa, indicating greater sensitivity than Ronan at that frequency. Low-frequency detection therefore differed among pinnipeds even though all subjects detected the lowest tested frequency. The calibrated thresholds identify the sound levels available to guide behavior in an underwater environment.')
    put(45,'Noise bandwidth changes low-frequency sound detection','Sills et al. (2025)',sills,'10.1121/10.0039049','Sills2025_f3','3 (200, 100, 80, and 63 Hz panels)','Detection improves as the masking noise band narrows.',
      '**Auditory masking** occurs when background sound raises the level needed to detect a signal. A **critical ratio** is the difference between the masked detection threshold and the noise spectral density level. At 200 Hz, Ronan’s critical ratio was 18 dB, with a masked threshold of 101 dB re 1 µPa.|The researchers also narrowed the noise band around the signal while maintaining its spectral density. Ronan’s detection improved with one-third-octave and one-sixth-octave noise relative to octave-wide noise. **Masking release** is this improvement when a masker becomes less effective; with the narrowest band, her threshold approached the unmasked threshold.|The results place Ronan’s **critical bandwidth**, the frequency range over which noise effectively masks the signal, above one-third of an octave at 200 Hz. The bearded seals instead retained nearly unchanged thresholds at this frequency, indicating narrower critical bands. Signal frequency, noise level, and noise bandwidth jointly determine whether an underwater acoustic cue can be detected.')
    return S
